<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n44532" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Smyrna</span></b>  (in the best MSS. <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Zmyr-na</span></b> ), ae, f., =
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Σμύρνα</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a celebrated maritime city of Ionia;</i>
			according to some, <i>the birthplace of Homer</i>,
			still called <i>Smyrna</i>, 
			<i><span class="en">Cic. Fl. 29, 71</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Agr.
				2, 15, 39</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Att. 9, 9, 2</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Liv. 33,
			36</span></i>; <i><span class="en">37, 35</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">Stat. S. 4, 2, 9 al.</span></i>— Hence,
				<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Smyrnaeus</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Zmyr-</span></b> ), a, um,
				adj., <i>of</i> or <i>belonging to Smyrna</i>, <i>Smyrnean</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sinus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Mel.
					1, 17, 3</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">conventus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					5, 29, 31, § 120</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vates,</span>”
				i. e. <i>Homer</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Luc.
					9, 984</span></i>
			; cf. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">of the same, plectra,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sil.
					8, 595</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">and, tubae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sid. Carm. 23, 131</span></i>
			.—<i>Plur. subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Smyr-naei</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Zmyr-</span></b> ),
			ōrum, m., <i>the
				inhabitants of Smyrna</i>, <i><span class="en">Cic. Arch. 8,
				19</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Liv. 37, 16, 8</span></i>.
			—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>An ancient name of Ephesus</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 5, 29,
				31, § 115</span></i>.</div>
